            <h2>Page 3</h2>
            <h1>Impacts & Solutions</h1>
            <h4>If left unaddressed the structural degradation of youth attention spans will severely impact global productivity, public health, and social togetherness by making it harder to build deep, unmediated relationships. However we must recognize that online spaces also provide marginalized and remote youth with life saving peer communities that actively reduce loneliness. To fix the broader dynamic, tech companies must be legally forced to remove addictive UI hooks like infinite scroll and schools much adopt mandatory digital literacy programs.</h4>
        </div>
        <div>
            <img class="small" src="static/imp&sol.jpg" alt="Internet Globe">
            <h4>Change also requires government action and personal responsibility especially since 47% of NZ teens and 77% of caregivers support raising the social media access age to 16. On an individual level we cannot wait for tech giants to change, we must protect our own human dignity by establishing digital free zones at home during family dinner and in bedrooms around 9pm. By utilizing device time limits turning screens to grayscale and choosing face to face interactions we can successfully reclaim our lives from the scroll</h4>
